{"cid:3151":{"key":"cid:3151","title":"Domperidone","compound":{"key":"cid:3151","cid":3151,"qid":"Q424238","title":"Domperidone","iupac":"6-chloro-3-[1-[3-(2-oxo-3H-benzimidazol-1-yl)propyl]piperidin-4-yl]-1H-benzimidazol-2-one","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domperidone","cas":"57808-66-9","iuphar":"965","drugbank":"DB01184","chemspider":"3039","unii":"5587267Z69","kegg":"D01745","chebi":"31515","chembl":"CHEMBL219916","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID1045116","echa":"100.055.408","description":"peripherally selective dopamine D2 receptor antagonist, used as an antiemetic, gastroprokinetic agent, and galactagogue","kind":null,"atc":"A03FA03","mesh":"D004294","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:28+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"C1CN(CCC1N2C3=C(C=C(C=C3)Cl)NC2=O)CCCN4C5=CC=CC=C5NC4=O","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:42:39+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"For management of dyspepsia, heartburn, epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01184","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Drugs used to prevent NAUSEA or VOMITING.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0001391.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Drugs that bind to but do not activate DOPAMINE RECEPTORS, thereby blocking the actions of dopamine or exogenous agonists. Many drugs used in the treatment of psychotic disorders (ANTIPSYCHOTIC AGENTS) are dopamine antagonists, although their therapeutic effects may be due to long-term adjustments of the brain rather than to the acute effects of blocking dopamine receptors. Dopamine antagonists have been used for several other clinical purposes including as ANTIEMETICS, in the treatment of Tourette syndrome, and for hiccup. Dopamine receptor blockade is associated with NEUROLEPTIC MALIGNANT SYNDROME.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0027734.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Milk, Human; Antiemetics; Dopamine Antagonists; Galactogogues; Gastrointestinal Agents","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM495/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Domperidone is a specific blocker of dopamine receptors. It speeds gastrointestinal peristalsis, causes prolactin release, and is used as antiemetic and tool in the study of dopaminergic mechanisms.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01184","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Domperidone acts as a gastrointestinal emptying (delayed) adjunct and peristaltic stimulant. The gastroprokinetic properties of domperidone are related to its peripheral dopamine receptor blocking properties. Domperidone facilitates gastric emptying and decreases small bowel transit time by increasing esophageal and gastric peristalsis and by lowering esophageal sphincter pressure. Antiemetic: The antiemetic properties of domperidone are related to its dopamine receptor blocking activity at both the chemoreceptor trigger zone and at the gastric level. It has strong affinities for the D2 and D3 dopamine receptors, which are found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, located just outside the blood brain barrier, which - among others - regulates nausea and vomiting","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01184","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"91%-93%","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01184","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Domperidone has known human metabolites that include 1-Propyl-1,3-dihydro-2H-benzimidazol-2-one, 3-[3-[4-(5-Chloro-2-oxo-3H-benzimidazol-1-yl)piperidin-1-yl]propyl]-6-hydroxy-1H-benzimidazol-2-one, and 5-Chloro-1-(piperidin-4-yl)-1H-benzo[d]imidazol-2(3H)-one.","source":"NORMAN Suspect List Exchange","url":null,"license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"7 hours","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01184","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation Domperidone is not approved for marketing in the United States by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) but is available in other countries. Domperidone may also be available from some compounding pharmacies and via the internet in the US. The quality of such products cannot be assured, and the FDA has warned against their use. Data available from 4 small studies on the excretion of domperidone into breastmilk are inconsistent, but infants would probably receive less than 0.1% of the maternal weight-adjusted dosage, even at high maternal doses. No adverse effects have been found in a limited number of published cases of breastfed infants whose mothers were taking domperidone. Domperidone is sometimes used as a galactogogue to increase milk supply. and has been used in induced lactation after surrogacy and by adoptive and transgender women. Galactogogues should never replace evaluation and counseling on modifiable factors that affect milk production. Most mothers who are provided instruction in good breastfeeding technique and breastfeed frequently are unlikely to obtain much additional benefit from domperidone. Several meta-analyses of domperidone use as a galactogogue in mothers of preterm infants concluded that domperidone can increase milk production acutely in the range of 90 to 94 mL daily. The most recent and largest meta-analysis concluded that domperidone is more effective than placebo as a galactogogue, but only in the mothers of preterm infants and that 30 mg daily for 7 days is the optimal dose. Other reviewers concluded that improvement of breastfeeding practices seems more effective and safer than off-label use of domperidone. One retrospective study found that breastmilk feeding rates at discharge remained consistently lower in very preterm (<32 weeks gestation) infants of women dispensed domperidone than those who were not prescribed domperidone. Domperidone has no officially established dosage for increasing milk supply in any country. Most published studies have used domperidone in a dosage of 10 mg 3 times daily for 4 to 10 days. Two small studies found no statistically significant additional increase in milk output with a dosage of 20 mg 3 times daily compared to a dosage of 10 mg 3 times and that women who failed to respond to the low dosage did not respond to the higher dosage. Dosages greater than 30 mg daily may increase the risk of arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death in patients receiving domperidone, although some feel that the risk is less in nursing mothers because of their relatively younger age. Large database retrospective cohort studies in Canada found an increase in the risk of cardiac arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death, but some confounders make the results subject to question. In one case, domperidone use uncovered congenital long QT syndrome in a woman who developed loss of consciousness, behavioral arrest, and jerking while taking domperidone. Mothers with a history of","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM495/"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"child":{"label":"Suitable for children","count":12,"forms":[["Oral Suspension",6],["Effervescent Granules",3],["Oro-dispersible tablet",1],["Effervescent granules in sachet",1],["Rectal Suppositories",1]]},"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":12,"forms":[["Oral Suspension",6],["Effervescent Granules",3],["Oro-dispersible tablet",1],["Effervescent granules in sachet",1],["Rectal Suppositories",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":0,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:3151","title":"Domperidone","products":22,"mw":425.9,"logp":3.35,"tpsa":78.8,"hbd":2,"hba":3,"rotb":5,"rings":5,"heavy":30,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H24ClN5O2"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:16362","title":"Pimozide","products":1,"mw":461.6,"logp":5.86,"tpsa":41.0,"hbd":1,"hba":2,"rotb":7,"rings":5,"heavy":34,"lipinski":1,"formula":"C28H29F2N3O","similarity":0.547},{"key":"cid:6918248","title":"Flibanserin","products":6,"mw":390.4,"logp":3.17,"tpsa":44.3,"hbd":1,"hba":3,"rotb":4,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C20H21F3N4O","similarity":0.406},{"key":"cid:60149","title":"Sertindole","products":4,"mw":441.0,"logp":4.63,"tpsa":40.5,"hbd":1,"hba":2,"rotb":5,"rings":5,"heavy":31,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C24H26ClFN4O","similarity":0.329},{"key":"cid:164522","title":"Bepotastine","products":5,"mw":388.9,"logp":4.17,"tpsa":62.7,"hbd":1,"hba":4,"rotb":8,"rings":3,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H25ClN2O3","similarity":0.308},{"key":"cid:4917","title":"Prochlorperazine","products":1,"mw":374.0,"logp":4.58,"tpsa":9.7,"hbd":0,"hba":4,"rotb":4,"rings":4